// A `!` on a module const asks for the one shape that can have real storage: a
// fixed-capacity global whose buffer is a plain `static`. The GC never allocated
// it, never moves it and never has to trace it — which holds exactly while every
// element is a VALUE (a primitive, `string` included since it is an interned u32
// atom, an enum, or a distinct over one).
//
// A struct / nested-array element would put a heap POINTER in that static buffer,
// and the collector's root set is the frame chain plus the defer stack: it would
// never see it, so the pointee would be freed — or moved, the young gen copies —
// with the global left dangling. T3076 on each, until a global root registry for
// object references exists.
//
// The marker is rejected at ANY level, not just the array's: `Spec![]` promises no
// more than `Spec[]!` does (writing through either is T3070 anyway, a module const
// has no path to write through), so it would only mislead.
module "snippet"

Spec :: struct { code: i32 }

SPECS:  Spec[]!:  [Spec { .code = 1 }]
GRID:   i32[][]!: [[1]]
ORIGIN: Spec!:    Spec { .code = 0 }
TPL:    Spec![]:  [Spec { .code = 2 }]

main :: fn() -> i32 = SPECS[0].code + GRID[0][0] + ORIGIN.code + TPL[0].code
